WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:03.058
Robots and cars are moving to central computers.

00:03.058 --> 00:05.988
Vision, planning and language models now share

00:05.988 --> 00:09.046
one chip, and they all want it at the same time.

00:09.846 --> 00:13.989
A robot camera gives you a frame every thirty-three milliseconds.

00:13.989 --> 00:15.773
Your detector needs fifteen.

00:15.773 --> 00:19.087
That fits, until something else wants the same chip.

00:19.087 --> 00:22.720
Now the detector waits behind a block it cannot interrupt

00:22.720 --> 00:26.799
and by the time its answer arrives, the robot has already moved.

00:27.499 --> 00:30.113
Vigilant sits in front of your inference

00:30.113 --> 00:33.445
server and asks one question before every dispatch:

00:33.445 --> 00:36.908
will this result still be useful when it is finished?

00:36.908 --> 00:40.567
Work that cannot be is dropped before it costs anything.

00:40.567 --> 00:42.331
Same protocol, same models.

00:42.331 --> 00:45.010
In your client, only the address changes.

00:45.710 --> 00:49.741
Four decisions, all made before a request reaches the chip.

00:49.741 --> 00:53.703
A newer frame replaces an older one that is still waiting.

00:53.703 --> 00:57.051
Work that would finish too late is never started.

00:57.051 --> 01:00.808
A long background job waits when protected work is due.

01:00.808 --> 01:04.975
And when time runs short, a smaller model variant takes over.

01:05.675 --> 01:08.544
It is built for exactly that kind of machine.

01:08.544 --> 01:12.624
A humanoid robot, whose vision must not wait behind its planner.

01:12.624 --> 01:15.685
Or a vehicle's central computer, running several

01:15.685 --> 01:19.064
cameras and a slower scene analysis on the same chip.

01:19.764 --> 01:22.499
Measured against a tuned Triton on the same GPU

01:22.499 --> 01:26.515
with a real detector and a background job that cannot be interrupted:

01:26.515 --> 01:29.018
the detector answers in time in ninety-nine

01:29.018 --> 01:31.870
percent of control cycles instead of eighty-five.

01:31.870 --> 01:33.790
Twenty times fewer missed cycles.

01:34.590 --> 01:36.807
And it is not tied to one machine.

01:36.807 --> 01:40.588
The same governor runs in front of Triton on an NVIDIA GPU

01:40.588 --> 01:45.412
and in front of TensorFlow Lite on the Adreno GPUs of two Android devices.

01:45.412 --> 01:50.823
On each of them, vig autotune measured the hardware and set the governor up for it.

01:51.623 --> 01:54.875
You do not have to take our numbers, or our settings.

01:54.875 --> 01:58.740
One command, vig autotune, measures your models on your machine

01:58.740 --> 02:01.931
tries the governor's settings against your contracts

02:01.931 --> 02:04.876
and only keeps what holds up when it runs again.

02:04.876 --> 02:07.637
On a Pixel 2 carrying a delivery robot's load

02:07.637 --> 02:11.257
the governor cut the detector's missed cycles by two thirds

02:11.257 --> 02:13.650
paid for by the lower-priority streams.

02:14.450 --> 02:17.735
Point it at the inference server you already run.

02:17.735 --> 02:21.826
Same protocol, same models, and your client changes one line.

02:21.826 --> 02:26.050
Free for evaluation, and for up to three devices in production.

02:26.750 --> 02:29.205
Faster chips compute the past faster.

02:29.205 --> 02:31.063
Vigilant stops computing it.

02:31.063 --> 02:33.916
Run vig autotune on the machine you already

02:33.916 --> 02:37.167
have, and let it tune the governor for your load.
